# Ask Meta, every morning, whether the credential we DELIVER through is still
# a credential.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. The Salla token died on 2026-07-29 and the first symptom
# anybody saw was paid orders provisioning nothing, nine days later. The
# cadence audit that followed found the Meta access token had no scheduled
# observer at all — the boot check asked whether WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN was a
# non-empty string, which is a question about a file, not about a credential.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO, and the reason is the whole design. It does NOT renew
# anything. A read-only GET /debug_token on 2026-08-08 says the live token is
# type SYSTEM_USER with expires_at 0 and data_access_expires_at 0: it cannot
# expire, and Meta publishes no refresh endpoint for it. A twin of
# career-salla-token.service would therefore be a scheduled job whose only
# possible outcome is failure, and a permanently red unit is a unit the
# operator learns to skip. This one watches the four things that CAN still
# take the token away without warning — revocation, a withdrawn scope, a phone
# number unassigned from the System User, and somebody pasting a temporary
# token over the permanent one — and says what to do about each.
#
# It sends no WhatsApp message and POSTs nothing anywhere: two read-only GETs,
# and the verdict is read off them. A watchdog that proves the channel by
# using it can bill us, can wake a customer, and can move the number's quality
# rating.
#
# Type=oneshot with OnFailure=, exactly like career-backup.service,
# career-engine-nightly.service and career-salla-token.service: a scheduled job
# that exits non-zero did not do its work, and career-alert@ puts that fact on
# the operator's phone once, deduplicated. The two messages are deliberately
# different — «the check could not run» and «the token is revoked and nobody
# is receiving anything» need different words and different frequencies, and
# the second comes from the script's own escalating alert, which speaks on a
# CHANGE and never on a repetition.
#
# EXIT CODES (scripts/check_whatsapp_token.py):
#   0  healthy, or a temporary token that still has more than a week of runway
#      (the finding is already on his phone once and in the nightly's boot
#      check daily; a unit sitting in `failed` for fifty-five days is a red
#      light that means «yes, we know»)
#   1  Meta could not be asked. Transient: no Telegram from the script, only
#      this unit's OnFailure=, because Meta's uptime is not our alarm
#   2  a human must generate a new token, re-grant a scope, or re-assign the
#      number
#   3  nothing configured to check
#
# EnvironmentFile carries WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN, WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID and
# the Telegram admin credentials. No DB_HOST/REDIS_HOST ride-along is needed:
# this job touches neither Postgres nor Redis — that is also why it must never
# import career.engine.cli (the D20 owner-role ratchet,
# tests/test_rls_runtime_role.py).
#
# NOT installed by anyone but Fahad: this file is the deliverable, and the
# `cp` + `daemon-reload` + `enable --now` is his gated step (scripts/gate.sh
# --host tells him whether the machine is running what this repository says,
# and will report this unit as missing until he does).
[Unit]
Description=Verify the Meta WhatsApp credential is still valid
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target
OnFailure=career-alert@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=/root/career
EnvironmentFile=/root/career/.env.staging
ExecStart=/root/career/.venv/bin/python /root/career/scripts/check_whatsapp_token.py
# The script retries its GET in-process (a read-only call rotates nothing, so
# unlike the Salla refresher it may safely ask again). This is the outer belt:
# a hung HTTPS call cannot pile runs up across days.
TimeoutStartSec=120
